WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.920
Here’s how you make a baby through sex

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.320
from the perspective of a chromosome

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.100
When mom makes an egg,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.200
her chromosomes double

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.220
Divide

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.220
And then divide again

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.620
Some end up in the egg

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.200
The rest are discarded into much smaller polar bodies

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.220
Now we have an egg, it meets a sperm,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
and it’s baby business time

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.177
Let’s go back to the part

00:00:23.177 --> 00:00:25.159
where the mom’s chromosomes separate

00:00:25.159 --> 00:00:28.220
It’s been a tenet that this process is random

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.749
This chromosome sometimes goes into the egg

00:00:30.749 --> 00:00:32.090
Sometimes into the polar body

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.430
Its homolog sometimes goes into the egg

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.620
Sometimes into the polar body

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.980
Recent studies now suggest this sorting

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.820
is not so random after all

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.070
Instead there might be a selfish gene at work

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.980
preferentially dragging

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.240
its home chromosome into the egg

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.210
and causing its match to get thrown away more often

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.040
The element is actually the centromere

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.340
-- a stretch of DNA in the chromosome

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.940
important for mechanically separating them

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.420
during cell division

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.379
It is the anchor point for the spindle--

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.040
the spot on the chromosome that gets pulled on

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.110
by the machinery of the cell

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.080
It turns out there are strong centromeres and weak ones

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.160
And the strong ones get passed down preferentially

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.400
How is this a selfish gene?

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.800
Well, centromeres are repetitive stretches of DNA

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.960
In fact ,centromeric repeats are the most abundant

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.060
non-coding elements in the human genome

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.120
Centromeres in preferentially inherited chromosomes in mice

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.240
have been shown to much bigger

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.840
--and they recruit more proteins

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.579
which are used to hook onto the spindle

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.119
These are the strong centromeres --

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:44.451
and they tend to end up pointing towards

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.980
the egg-end of the cell when it's dividing

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.724
But strong centromeres

00:01:48.724 --> 00:01:50.400
does not mean stronger attachment --

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.780
Instead, they are more likely to let go

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.380
of their spindle attachment,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.319
if it is pulling them towards the polar-body side

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.980
-- away from the egg-to-be-side.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.240
Because this mechanism depends on

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.000
tension between the 2 chromosomes,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.540
when the strong one lets go,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.440
the weak one, has to let go too

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.420
It’s like restarting a game if you’re
not winning

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.820
So, eventually, with enough stops and starts,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.760
the strong centromere is reoriented,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.740
heading for the egg side and possible inheritance

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:26.090
and the weak side is heading for the dumpster

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.730
How does the centromere know which way it's going?

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.720
The thought is the stronger centromere

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.310
listens in on a signal broadcast by the membrane,

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:40.010
that is intended to help the cell divide asymmetrically

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.360
the selfish centromere takes advantage of this signal

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.180
to recognize which way it is getting dragged

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.657
Why aren't we seeing bigger and bigger centromeres

00:02:48.657 --> 00:02:51.100
if they are preferentially placed into the egg?

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.320
It turns out that what’s good for these genes

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.720
might not be great for the egg or the body

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.360
-- making these strong centromeres truly selfish
genes

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.920
When researchers have scanned

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.620
the genome for rapidly evolving proteins

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.913
-- centromere-binders --

00:03:05.913 --> 00:03:08.660
those that attach to the centromere for some reason

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.500
-- are amongst the fastest

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.820
possibly because the rest of the genome is fighting back

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.000
So the question is--

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.560
How selfish are your centromeres?

